INT. DEFUNCT TAXIDERMY MUSEUM - NIGHT

Glass eyes glint. A stuffed bear looms. Moonlight cuts through blinds.

DETECTIVE LOUISA FAIRWEATHER-KNOX, 40s, stands beside a toppled display case. DET. SANJAY PILLAI, 30s, photographs the glass. PROFESSOR ISADORE WREN, 70s, wrings his hands. MS. PRISCILLA OYEDEJI, 50s, the curator, leans on a pedestal. A night watchman, ELLIOTT CRUZ, 20s, stands guard by the door.

LOUISA
Who last saw the owl?

PRISCILLA
I did. Six o'clock.

ISADORE
Seven.

PRISCILLA
Six, Isadore.

ISADORE
You left at six. I was still here at seven.

SANJAY
So you saw it at seven.

ISADORE
I saw something at seven.

LOUISA
Something?

ISADORE
A shape. In the dark.

ELLIOTT
There's always a shape, Professor.

ISADORE
This one moved.

PRISCILLA
Everything moves if you stare long enough.

LOUISA
Quiet.

She points her flashlight at the empty display case. The glass is broken outward, not in. She crouches and picks up a single gray feather.

LOUISA (CONT'D)
It wasn't stolen. It left.

SANJAY
Owls don't leave. They're stuffed.

ISADORE
That one wasn't.

Elliott's radio crackles. Static. Then a soft, rhythmic hooting. Everyone turns toward the dark rafters.

PRISCILLA
Oh no.

ISADORE
Oh yes.

Louisa shines the light upward. Two enormous yellow eyes gleam. A shadow unfolds.
